WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.020
Film Courage: In a romantic comedy it seems
like each character, each partner of this

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.420
“dance” is missing something in their
lives.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:13.670
And they see (at some point) this other person
will basically cure them of this missing piece.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:18.730
As viewers, why do we resonate with this them
and why is it so common in romantic comedies?

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:26.849
Pamela Jaye Smith: There is another wonderful
greek myth that says that originally humans

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:29.849
were both male and female together.

00:00:29.849 --> 00:00:36.250
And we had four legs and four arms and two
heads and we were joined.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:41.230
But with all those limbs, humans were able
to start climbing up Mount Olympus and were

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:44.580
able to start challenging the gods.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:53.210
And God said “Oh….this will never do!”
and so they tore humans apart.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:56.930
And male and female were separated.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:05.050
And since then it is the nature of our yearning
to try to reunite into the one form.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.580
So that is how a mythology set it up.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.640
What we are trying to do in romance is find
that missing part of ourselves.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:20.620
What I think is interesting is that some people
are trying to find that thing that will compliment

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.250
them because they don’t have it.

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:27.780
And then there are those instances where somebody
says “Oh you’ve got that so much more

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:28.780
than I do.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.130
I’m going to look a whole lot better than
you by comparison.

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:36.700
If I’m cynical but you’re more cynical,
that’s going to make me look like the good

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:37.700
guy!”

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.240
So some of those romantic comedies have that
sort of mechanism in it.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:49.060
But I think basically what most of us are
looking for in our romantic situations is

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.550
a completion.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:58.060
What we see in our romantic relationships
is often said to be a reflection of our relationship

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:09.450
with our higher spiritual selves or with the
cosmos that we connect to life through each

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.450
other.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:16.760
That we connect to the one life by relating
closely to one person.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.980
And so the alchemy of love can actually, truly
lift us up.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.980
And that this is what a lot of us are looking
for, even if we’re not conscious of it.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:36.360
But we are looking for that bliss, that nirvana
within our love relationships.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:43.990
And you may have heard some version of this
where people say “We kissed and the world

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.690
disappeared and we became Gods.”

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:50.080
And you go “Oh, yes!

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:51.110
Please!

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:53.160
That’s wonderful.”

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:59.190
One of my favorite authors Lawrence Durrell
(the British author) of The Alexandria Quartet,

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.590
which is all about love.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:07.520
And one of his characters makes a note and
says “In loving Justine I was loving all

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:12.950
women because she stood for the female.”

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:14.690
And so I think that is what we do, too.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.100
Even if we don’t realize it and we go “I
love you!”

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.270
we are also loving the essence of love.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:24.840
Film Courage: Why so often do opposites attract?

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:30.910
Why is it so often their is a play between
the hero worshipper or the shy admirer?

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:38.240
And the Type A sort of…I mean I’m thinking
of the movie JERRY MAGUIRE.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:39.240
Pamela Jaye Smith: Yes!

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.349
Film Courage: Which film I absolutely love.

00:03:42.349 --> 00:03:43.569
But this is a common scenario.

00:03:43.569 --> 00:03:45.629
Whether one is the male or one is the female.

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:49.270
You can switch roles as to who is the hero
worshipper.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:51.700
Why do we as viewers love this scenario so
much?

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:59.239
Pamela Jaye Smith: I think we have to go back
to evolutionary biology and evolutionary psychology

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:01.239
to truly answer that.

00:04:01.239 --> 00:04:06.739
In small groups of people, you do not want
to have too much interbreeding because it’s

00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:07.739
not healthy.

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:10.099
So you need to stir up the gene pool.

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:14.190
And so there’s a phrase ‘The Brother or
the Other.’

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:18.879
You’re going to get a better immune system
and offspring if you’re not mating with

00:04:18.879 --> 00:04:25.530
your relatives (if you go for the other, that
which is different).

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:31.270
So you take that on up to modern times and
we’re still being influenced by that.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.080
We still see the other.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:38.030
The person who is different from us as more
of a potential mate.

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:46.969
A lot of that is just our biology psychology
from 200,000 years still working on us.

00:04:46.969 --> 00:04:53.259
But I also think it is part of the wonderful
magnetism of passion and of sex.

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:57.749
Regardless of what gender the body is, to
have the positive pole and the negative pole,

00:04:57.749 --> 00:05:00.020
that’s when you get magnetism.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.190
If you get two positive poles together, what
happens?

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:04.190
Nothing!

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:05.210
They repel each other.

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:10.020
But you get positive and negative together
and then you get sparks.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.279
You get the magnetic pull.

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:20.089
So I think that part of that opposites attract
is built into human nature.

00:05:20.089 --> 00:05:24.419
That is trying to produce the best offspring.

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:26.840
And part of it is also just our sense of adventure.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.810
Film Courage: Let’s take the (1976) movie
ANNIE HALL.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:32.800
So you have Woody Allen’s character (Alvy
Singer) who is self-effacing, he is down on

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:36.379
himself and then you have Diane Keaton who
is a breath of fresh air.

00:05:36.379 --> 00:05:38.119
She’s in a great mood.

00:05:38.119 --> 00:05:40.680
She’s very open to life.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.219
Why does this dynamic work?

00:05:42.219 --> 00:05:48.279
Pamela Jaye Smith: Well I would counter and
say it didn’t actually work because they

00:05:48.279 --> 00:05:50.490
broke up.

00:05:50.490 --> 00:06:00.589
And she, in spite of her seeming self-confidence,
also had some problems with her own self-worth

00:06:00.589 --> 00:06:05.740
and what she wanted and what she thought she
should want.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:06.740
But yes.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.169
On the surface they are opposites.

00:06:08.169 --> 00:06:12.710
And they attract and it’s fun and that is
one of the most fun parts about that romantic

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:13.710
comedy.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:20.300
But if you look at it again, I think you’ll
see that they are a whole lot more alike at

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.530
heart, than they are different.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.430
I was surprised at that when I went back and
watched it.

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:27.400
With an analytical eye for the book (Romantic
Comedies: These Films Can Save Your Love Life!).

